-- Model answers for the 24 prompts of the Writing practice tests PT-WRITING-01 to PT-WRITING-12 (part 1 is Task 1, part 2 is Task 2).
--
-- Same format as V68: one paragraph per part of the essay in order (Introduction, Overview, Body 1, Body 2 for Task 1;
-- Introduction, Body 1, Body 2, Conclusion for Task 2), separated by a blank line, with the phrases worth learning between
-- double asterisks. Task 1 answers use only the figures and facts given in the prompt, and every answer is longer than the
-- minimum of its task (150 and 250 words). The text is original and still needs a teacher's review. Until now these prompts
-- held the placeholder "Graded by the essay grader." in explanation; the grader never reads this column.

WITH samples (pkg_code, part_no, sample) AS (VALUES
('PT-WRITING-01', 1, $sample$
The line graph illustrates **the proportion of households** that owned four types of electronic device between 1995 and 2015.

Overall, ownership of mobile phones, computers and tablets rose over the period, whereas MP3 players were the only item to **show a decline** after reaching a peak. By 2015, mobile phones were the most common device, followed by computers, tablets and MP3 players, in that order.

In 1995, computers were more common than mobile phones, at 20% compared with 5%. Mobile phones then **grew rapidly** to reach 95% by 2015, finishing above computers, which rose more slowly to 85%. In other words, a gap of 15 percentage points in favour of computers had become a gap of 10 points in favour of mobile phones.

Tablets were not owned at all until around 2010, but they then climbed to 60% by the end of the period. MP3 players followed a different pattern, as they **reached a peak** of 45% in 2010 before falling to only 20% in 2015, so that tablets were owned by three times as many households as MP3 players at the end.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-01', 2, $sample$
Remote work has become far more common in recent years, and some people claim that it benefits both **employees and employers**. I largely agree with this view, although it suits some jobs better than others and it is not free of problems.

For workers, the main advantage is **a better work-life balance**. Employees who stay at home save the time and money they would otherwise spend on commuting, so they can use those hours to rest, exercise or look after their families. Many people also say they are more productive in a quiet environment, free from the constant interruptions of an open-plan office, and they enjoy the freedom to organise their day in the way that suits them best. This independence often makes staff feel trusted and more loyal to their company.

Employers benefit as well. Companies that allow remote work can **cut overhead costs** such as rent and electricity, and they can recruit talented people who live far away from the office instead of only those in the same city. For example, several technology firms have closed their offices and still **maintain high output**. However, some tasks need teamwork in person, and new staff may find it hard to learn without direct guidance from colleagues, so managers must support them carefully.

In conclusion, working from home is usually good for both sides, mainly because it saves time and money and gives people more control over their lives. Nevertheless, firms should offer **a flexible mix** of home and office days so that collaboration between colleagues is not lost.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-02', 1, $sample$
The bar chart compares **the average number of hours** that students in five countries spent studying each week in 2020.

Overall, students in Korea studied for the longest time by a clear margin, while those in Brazil studied for the shortest, and study time ranged from 9 to 18 hours a week. Japan and Korea both **recorded higher figures** than the other three countries.

Korean students spent 18 hours a week studying, which was four hours more than in Japan, the next highest country, at 14 hours. These two countries were the only ones where students studied for more than 12 hours a week, so the gap between them and the rest was clear.

The remaining three countries had lower figures. Canada stood at 12 hours, followed by Germany with 11 hours, so students in these two countries studied for **a very similar amount** of time. Brazil was **at the bottom of the ranking** with just 9 hours, which was exactly half the figure for Korea and nine hours below it.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-02', 2, $sample$
Whether university education should be free is a topic that **divides opinion**. Some people believe the state should pay for it, while others think students should cover the cost themselves. I will discuss both views before giving my own opinion.

Those who support free education argue that it gives everyone **an equal opportunity** to succeed. If tuition is free, talented students from poor families can still attend university, and the country gains more doctors, engineers and teachers. Students also finish their studies without a heavy burden of debt, which lets them start their careers, buy a home and **make a contribution to society** earlier than they otherwise could. Countries such as Germany, for example, have long offered low-cost university places for this reason.

On the other hand, others argue that students should pay because they are the ones who benefit most from a degree, as graduates usually earn higher salaries. Free places also require large amounts of public money, which could be used for other services such as hospitals, and when a course costs nothing some students may **take their studies less seriously** and leave without finishing. If the state pays for every place, universities may also struggle to keep their quality high.

In my opinion, a mixed system is the fairest solution. Governments should pay for most of the cost, particularly for students with low incomes, while those who can afford it contribute a fair amount, perhaps after they graduate and find a job. In this way, university remains open to everyone without placing the whole cost on taxpayers.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-03', 1, $sample$
The two pie charts illustrate **the proportion of energy** that an average household used for four purposes in 1990 and in 2020.

Overall, heating remained the largest use of energy in both years, although its share fell. The most notable change was the rise in the energy used by appliances, which **more than doubled**, while lighting dropped sharply. As a result, the four uses became more evenly balanced in 2020 than they had been in 1990.

In 1990, heating accounted for just over half of household energy, at 52%, followed by lighting at 20% and appliances at 18%. Cooking took **the smallest share**, at 10%. Heating and lighting together made up 72% of the total.

By 2020, heating had fallen by twelve percentage points to 40%, and lighting had declined to only 8%, so that these two uses together accounted for 48%. By contrast, appliances rose to 38%, which was **almost equal to** heating, and cooking grew slightly to 14%.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-03', 2, $sample$
Traffic congestion is becoming a serious issue in many cities around the world. This essay will examine the problems it causes and suggest some **practical measures** that could reduce it.

The most obvious problem is the amount of time that people lose. Commuters who spend hours in slow traffic are tired and less productive, and delivery vehicles arrive late, which raises the cost of goods and services. Congestion also causes **air pollution**, because engines that run for long periods in a queue release large quantities of harmful gases, and this damages the health of residents, especially children and elderly people. Noise and stress are further consequences for people who live near busy roads. Emergency vehicles can also be delayed in heavy traffic, which puts lives at risk.

Several steps could help to solve the problem. First, governments should invest in **reliable public transport**, such as buses, metro lines and trams, so that people have a convenient and affordable alternative to driving. Second, cities can charge drivers a fee for entering the centre at busy times, as London has done, which encourages people to travel at other times or to share cars. Finally, safe cycle lanes and wider pavements could persuade people to cycle or walk for short journeys. Such measures would also make streets safer and quieter for residents, which improves their quality of life.

In conclusion, congestion wastes time, harms health and increases costs, but a combination of better public transport, road charges and cycling facilities could **ease the pressure** on city roads and make urban life more pleasant.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-04', 1, $sample$
The table compares **the percentage of people** in four age groups who went to the cinema at least once a month in 2000, 2010 and 2020.

Overall, cinema attendance dipped among the two youngest groups but grew among the oldest. People aged 15 to 24 were the most frequent visitors throughout, whereas the 50 and over group was the least frequent in all three years, although it was the only group to show **a steady rise**.

The youngest group had the highest figures: 48% in 2000, 52% in 2010 and 40% in 2020. The 25 to 34 group **followed a similar pattern** at a lower level, rising from 35% to 38% before dropping to 33%.

Older groups were less likely to go to the cinema, but their figures changed differently. For the 35 to 49 group, attendance was 22% in 2000, rose to 25% and then edged down to 24%. The 50 and over group grew from 10% to 14% and then to 18%, which means it **almost doubled** over the twenty years.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-04', 2, $sample$
More and more students are choosing to complete their studies in another country. In my opinion, the advantages of this trend **outweigh the disadvantages**, although the disadvantages should not be ignored.

The main benefit is the quality of education and the experience that students gain. Many foreign universities offer **world-class facilities** and courses that are not available at home, and a degree from a respected institution can improve job prospects. Living abroad also helps young people to become independent, to improve their language skills and to understand other cultures, and these qualities are highly valued by employers in an increasingly global economy. For example, a graduate who has studied in an English-speaking country often finds it easier to join an international company.

Nevertheless, there are some drawbacks. Studying overseas is expensive because students must pay tuition fees and living costs, and families may find it hard to afford them. In addition, students may feel **homesick** and lonely, and being far from their relatives can affect their mental health and their results. Some universities reduce this problem by offering counselling and welcome programmes for international students. There is also the risk that talented graduates will not return, which means that their home country loses skilled workers, a problem often called brain drain. Governments can address this by creating attractive jobs for returning graduates.

In conclusion, studying abroad can be costly and difficult emotionally, but the academic, personal and professional benefits are greater. With careful planning and support from universities, most students can **make the most of** the opportunity.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-05', 1, $sample$
The diagram illustrates **the process** by which tea bags are produced, from the picking of the leaves to the packing of the finished product.

Overall, the process consists of seven stages and is a linear one, in which each stage follows directly from the one before it. The first four stages deal with the leaves themselves, while the last three **prepare the product** for sale.

First, tea leaves are picked. They are then withered and rolled, and after that the rolled leaves are dried. At this point, the raw material has been **fully processed** and is ready to be sorted, so the leaves have changed form several times before they reach the next stage.

In the fifth stage, the dried leaves are sorted by size. Next, they are packed into paper bags, which is the point at which the tea becomes a tea bag. In the final stage the bags are placed in boxes, which **completes the process**.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-05', 2, $sample$
Nowadays, fewer young people read newspapers or watch the news on television. This essay will explain the reasons for this trend and argue that it **is a problem** for society.

There are several reasons for this change. Firstly, young people spend most of their time on smartphones, where they get information from social media and video platforms instead of traditional news. These sources are free, fast and **available at any time**, so there is little reason to buy a paper or wait for a bulletin. In addition, many platforms show short clips that fit easily into a commute or a lunch break. Secondly, many young people find news programmes boring or too negative, so they prefer entertainment, and busy students and workers rarely have time to sit down and read a long article.

I believe this is a problem for society. People who do not follow the news are **less informed** about politics and the economy, and this may lead them to vote without understanding the issues or to ignore important debates in their community. Moreover, social media often spreads false stories, so young people who rely on it may believe **misleading information**, which can increase division between groups and reduce trust in institutions. In the long term, a population that ignores reliable reporting is easier to mislead.

In conclusion, young people read the news less often because online media is more convenient and more entertaining. However, a society needs informed citizens, so schools and news organisations should work together to make reliable news more attractive and accessible to young people.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-06', 1, $sample$
The two maps illustrate **the changes** that have taken place in a village centre between 1995 and the present day.

Overall, the village has become more built up. Farmland and the pond have disappeared, and new housing and a car park have appeared, while one building has **changed its use** from a school to a library. The centre therefore has more buildings and less green space than it had in 1995.

The most significant change concerns land use. The farmland that existed in 1995 has been replaced by housing, and the pond has been filled in, so there is **less open space** and water in the village today. These two changes show that the village has expanded to provide more homes.

Two other developments are worth noting. The old school has been converted into a library, so the building remains but serves a different purpose. In addition, a new car park has been built beside the station, an area that did not contain one in 1995, so people can now leave their vehicles close to the railway.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-06', 2, $sample$
Some people think that children should begin to learn a foreign language at primary school. I strongly agree with this opinion, because early learning offers several **clear benefits**.

First of all, young children learn languages more easily than adults. Their brains are flexible, and they can **pick up pronunciation** and basic grammar naturally, simply by listening and playing games in the classroom. A child who starts at the age of six will usually speak with more confidence and a better accent than a teenager who begins later, and this makes learning at secondary school much easier because the foundations are already in place. They also gain confidence in speaking, since they are not afraid of making mistakes at that age.

In addition, knowing a second language has wider advantages. It encourages children to be curious about other cultures and helps them to **develop an open mind**. In later life, it also improves job opportunities in a global economy, where employers value staff who can communicate with international customers and colleagues. Some studies even suggest that bilingual children are better at solving problems and concentrating in class. This shows that language learning supports other subjects as well.

Of course, there are some challenges, such as the lack of qualified teachers and a crowded timetable. However, these can be solved through teacher training and short, enjoyable lessons that use songs and games. Parents can also help by encouraging children to practise at home. In conclusion, I believe that starting a foreign language at primary school is **a worthwhile investment** that benefits both children and society.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-07', 1, $sample$
The table compares **the average number of visits** per person to public libraries in one year, by age group, in 2000 and in 2020.

Overall, library use fell in four of the five age groups, and the 65 and over group was the only one to **show an increase**. This group also visited libraries most often in both years, while 16 to 24 year-olds were the least frequent visitors.

People aged 65 and over made 11 visits in 2000, and this rose slightly to 12 in 2020. By contrast, under-16s went from 9 visits to 6, and 45 to 64 year-olds from 7 to 5, which were **the two largest falls**, of three and two visits respectively.

The other two groups had lower figures. The 16 to 24 group visited 4 times in 2000 and 3 times in 2020, while the 25 to 44 group went from 5 visits to 4, so each of them **fell by one visit**.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-07', 2, $sample$
Some people think that schools should spend more time teaching practical skills, such as cooking and managing money, and less time on academic subjects. I partly agree with this view, but I do not think academic subjects should be **reduced significantly**.

On the one hand, practical skills prepare young people for adult life. Many graduates cannot cook a healthy meal, draw up a budget or understand how loans work, and this leads to **poor financial decisions** and an unhealthy diet. If schools taught these skills, students would leave education more independent and better able to look after themselves and their families, and they would feel more confident when they live alone for the first time. They can also help their parents by preparing meals and managing the household budget.

On the other hand, academic subjects such as mathematics, science and languages build **critical thinking** and give students the knowledge they need for university and for most professional careers. Without a strong academic foundation, young people would have fewer options when they choose a job. In addition, practical skills can be learned at home or through short courses, whereas complex subjects are difficult to study alone. For this reason, teachers should not give up the time that complex topics require.

In conclusion, I believe schools should teach practical skills, but as an addition to the academic curriculum, not a replacement. A **balanced timetable**, with a few hours a week for cooking and money management, would give students the best preparation for life. In this way, they leave school with both knowledge and independence.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-08', 1, $sample$
The line graph compares **the average monthly rainfall**, in millimetres, in Lisbon and Dublin over the course of a year.

Overall, rainfall in Dublin was fairly even throughout the year, whereas Lisbon had **far greater variation**, with very wet winters and an extremely dry summer. The two cities therefore had very different patterns, even though neither had a month without rain.

Dublin's rainfall stayed within a narrow range, between 50 mm and 85 mm. It reached its lowest point of 50 mm in April and its peak of 85 mm in November, so the difference between its driest and wettest months was only 35 mm.

Lisbon, by contrast, started the year at 100 mm in January, then fell to a low of just 5 mm in July. After that it **rose sharply** and finished at 110 mm in December, the highest figure of either city, which means that the gap between its driest and wettest months was 105 mm. In summer, therefore, Lisbon was much drier than Dublin.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-08', 2, $sample$
Some people believe that governments should fund museums and theatres, while others think this money should be spent on healthcare and education. I will discuss both views and explain why I believe that **essential services** should have priority.

Supporters of funding for the arts argue that museums and theatres preserve a nation's heritage and **enrich public life**. Ticket prices would be too high for ordinary families without public support, so only wealthy people could enjoy culture. These places also attract tourists and create jobs, so the money spent on them can return to the economy, and they give children the chance to learn about history and the arts outside school. For example, a school visit to a history museum can make lessons far more memorable.

However, others argue that hospitals and schools are far more important. Healthcare saves lives, and education gives young people the skills they need to find work, so these services **directly affect** the quality of life of every citizen. Government budgets are limited, and when hospitals are short of staff or classrooms are overcrowded, spending money on a theatre seems difficult to justify to the taxpayers who fund it. Poor healthcare also reduces the number of people able to work, which harms the economy as well.

In my opinion, healthcare and education should receive the larger share of public money, because basic needs come first. Nevertheless, governments should still provide some funding for culture, perhaps combined with **private sponsorship**, so that the arts remain available to everyone. This approach would respect both the cultural and the practical needs of society.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-09', 1, $sample$
The diagram illustrates **the stages** involved in making recycled paper from waste paper.

Overall, the process consists of seven stages. It begins with the collection and sorting of waste paper and ends with the finished paper being rolled up and cut to size, and it involves **mixing, cleaning, pressing and drying** along the way. Water and chemicals are used in the early and middle stages, while heat is applied in the sixth stage.

At the first stage, waste paper is collected and sorted. It is then mixed with water in a large tank, where it is broken down into pulp. The pulp passes through screens, which remove staples and plastic, and ink is washed out with chemicals, so that only clean pulp remains.

Once it is clean, the pulp is pressed between rollers to form wet sheets. These sheets are then dried by heated cylinders in the sixth stage. **In the final stage**, the paper is rolled up and cut to size, and the recycled paper is complete.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-09', 2, $sample$
More and more students are now taking university courses entirely online instead of attending classes on campus. In my view, the advantages of this development **outweigh the disadvantages**, although it does not suit everyone.

The biggest advantage is flexibility. Online students can study at any time and from any place, which makes it possible for people who work or **care for children** to earn a degree. Courses are often cheaper because there are no travel or accommodation costs, and students from remote areas can reach universities that would otherwise be too far away. Recorded lectures also allow learners to **study at their own pace** and repeat difficult material as often as they need. Teachers can also use discussion forums and video calls to answer questions quickly.

Nevertheless, there are some drawbacks. Learning online requires strong self-discipline, and without a fixed timetable some students fall behind or give up. Feeling isolated at home can also reduce motivation. In addition, they miss the social side of university life, such as group discussions and friendships, and subjects that need laboratory work or practical training are difficult to teach through a screen. Employers may also doubt that online graduates have practical experience.

In conclusion, online courses give more people access to higher education at a lower cost and with greater freedom, and these benefits are more important than the lack of personal contact. Universities should therefore continue to develop them while offering **some face-to-face support** for students who need it. In this way, the strengths of both methods can be combined.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-10', 1, $sample$
The two maps illustrate **the development** of a seaside town between 1990 and the present day.

Overall, the town has become larger and more developed. The fishing industry has disappeared, and the farmland and the beachside car park have been replaced by **new developments**, so the town now offers more for visitors and for residents than it did in 1990.

In the east, the fishing harbour has become a marina with cafes, and the fish market on the harbour road has closed. In the south, the car park beside the beach has been replaced by a promenade and a beach cafe, which means that the east and south of the town now serve visitors rather than fishing or parking.

In the north, the small hotel has **grown into** a large resort, and a new bypass runs around the north of the town. To the west, the farmland has become **a housing estate**, so that part of the town is now residential.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-10', 2, $sample$
The number of overweight children is rising in many countries. This essay will discuss the main causes of this problem and suggest some **effective solutions**.

There are two main causes. The first is an unhealthy diet. Fast food, sugary drinks and snacks are cheap and heavily advertised, and many busy parents rely on **processed meals** instead of cooking fresh food at home. As a result, children eat more fat, salt and sugar than they need. The second cause is a lack of exercise, because children now spend hours at a screen, playing games or watching videos, and they walk and play outside less than previous generations did. Many parents also worry about safety, so they do not allow their children to play outside alone.

Several measures could help to solve the problem. Schools should serve healthy meals, teach children about nutrition and provide **regular physical education** lessons so that exercise becomes a habit. Parents can also set limits on screen time and encourage the whole family to eat together and go out to play at weekends. Doctors recommend that children should be active for at least an hour every day. Finally, governments could restrict advertising for junk food aimed at children and place a tax on sugary drinks, as some countries have done. Such policies make healthy food cheaper in comparison and unhealthy food less attractive.

In conclusion, poor diet and inactivity are the main reasons why more children are overweight. By working together, schools, families and governments can **tackle the problem** and help children to build healthier lifestyles that will last into adult life.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-11', 1, $sample$
The bar chart compares **the percentage of electricity** produced from renewable sources in four countries in 2000 and in 2020.

Overall, the share of renewable electricity rose in all four countries. Denmark showed the most dramatic increase, while Canada had the highest figure in both years but **the smallest growth**. By 2020, Canada and Denmark were the only countries where renewable sources produced more than 60% of electricity.

Canada started with 60% in 2000 and reached 68% in 2020, an increase of only eight percentage points. Denmark, which had 17% in 2000, rose to 62%, **an increase of** 45 points, and so it almost caught up with Canada.

Spain's figure grew by 24 points, from 20% to 44%. Australia had the lowest share in 2000, at 8%, and although it rose by 21 points to 29% by 2020, it remained the lowest of the four countries. Even so, its growth was faster than that of Canada.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-11', 2, $sample$
Many people now work long hours and have little free time. This essay will explain the reasons for this trend and argue that it is mainly **a negative development**.

There are several reasons why people work so much. Firstly, the cost of living, especially housing, has increased, so many families need **extra income** and take on overtime or a second job. Secondly, modern technology means that employees are always available, because emails and messages follow them home, and it is difficult to switch off. Finally, competition for jobs is strong, and workers fear that they may lose their position if they leave on time while their colleagues stay late. In some countries, a culture of working late is even seen as a sign of commitment.

In my opinion, this trend is mostly negative. Long hours cause stress and tiredness, which can lead to **serious health problems**, and people who are exhausted are often less productive. They make more mistakes and need more time to finish the same tasks. Workers also have less time for their families, friends and hobbies, and this harms their relationships and their happiness. Some people argue that hard work brings higher salaries and career success, which is true, but these benefits rarely compensate for a poor quality of life. Money cannot replace time spent with loved ones.

In conclusion, people work long hours because of financial pressure, technology and competition. Employers and governments should therefore promote **reasonable working hours** so that employees can enjoy a healthier balance between work and rest.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-12', 1, $sample$
The two pie charts show **how household waste** was handled in one city in 2000 and in 2020.

Overall, the city changed from relying mainly on landfill to relying mainly on recycling. Landfill fell dramatically, recycling and composting **grew significantly**, and incineration changed only slightly. Landfill and incineration together handled 80% of the waste in 2000 but only 40% in 2020.

In 2000, landfill was by far the most common method, at 70%, while recycling accounted for 15%, incineration for 10% and composting for just 5%. Recycling and composting together handled only 20% of the waste at that time.

By 2020, landfill had dropped by 42 percentage points to 28%, and recycling had become the main method at 42%, which was **nearly three times** its earlier figure. Composting rose to 18%, and incineration increased slightly to 12%, so recycling and composting together now handled 60% of the city's waste. This shows how much the city's approach had changed in twenty years.
$sample$),
('PT-WRITING-12', 2, $sample$
Some people believe that robots and automation will take over most jobs in the future, leaving many people without work. I partly agree that many jobs will disappear, but I do not think that **mass unemployment** is inevitable.

It is true that machines are already replacing people in some sectors. Factories use robots to assemble cars, supermarkets have self-service tills, and software can now perform tasks such as **data entry** much faster than humans. Jobs that involve simple, repetitive work are therefore at risk, and workers in these roles may find it hard to find new employment, especially if they are older and have few qualifications. Companies may also prefer machines because they do not need breaks or holidays.

However, history shows that technology also creates new opportunities. When earlier machines appeared, they removed some jobs but **generated many others**, such as engineers, programmers and technicians who build and repair the equipment. In addition, there are tasks that machines cannot do well, including caring for patients, teaching and creative work, which require empathy and imagination and are likely to remain in human hands for a long time. Even so, machines may still help people in these fields by handling routine paperwork.

In conclusion, automation will change the job market and will remove many routine positions, but it will also create new roles. Governments and companies should invest in **retraining programmes** so that workers can learn new skills and benefit from technological progress instead of fearing it. Education systems should also teach digital skills from an early age.
$sample$)
)
UPDATE question_versions qv
SET explanation = s.sample
FROM samples s
JOIN content_packages p ON p.code = s.pkg_code
JOIN content_sections cs ON cs.package_version_id = p.current_published_version_id AND cs.part_no = s.part_no
JOIN section_questions sq ON sq.section_id = cs.id
WHERE qv.id = sq.question_version_id
  AND qv.answer_spec ->> 'type' = 'ESSAY';
